abstract: >-
  A contamination-resistant synthetic benchmark for retrieval-backed agent memory, plus the
  findings it produced and the tooling that keeps them honest. Eleven metrics (conversion,
  chain-fragility, distraction, fact-retention-at-budget, outcome-ranked recall, forget-precision,
  echo-resistance, compression-vs-raw, operational-continuity, temporal-as-of,
  integrity-conditioned recall), each with a pre-registered falsifier and bootstrap CIs, over a
  frozen sha256-pinned dataset of random-token entities (closed-book accuracy 0.000 by
  construction). Headline results: one missing hop collapses a multi-hop answer (chain-fragility
  +1.000, CI [1,1], n=200, two model families); ranking recall by was-it-right beats
  was-it-recalled (+0.36 to +0.47 lift, every CI excludes zero); re-stating a retired value
  resurrects it in a keyed store (echo-resistance 0.00) unless an object-keyed ledger refuses
  the echo (1.00). The benchmark audits itself: memaudit.py reports the shortcut floor reachable
  by surface-form rules alone, scored against permuted labels; on RAMR's own traces it read 97.2%
  and the balanced re-cut brought it to 40.0%, with both leaks it caught recorded in ERRATA.md.
  ramr_preflight.py runs four admissibility gates (budget parity, retrieval, liveness, parameter
  efficacy) and aborts by naming the layer that died. The integrity/ module runs the same
  questions against real stores (mem0, Zep/Graphiti, inspeximus) through one ground-truth-blind
  judge. Every cited number is recomputed from its persisted source by verify_numbers.py.
